From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 22:34:41 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Ship a Durable Object's committed WAL frames to the node and
 hold its answers until they are confirmed

Coworkerd v61, depends on v39's inherited descriptors (0008). A Durable Object's data no longer
stays on the node that owns its shard. The localDisk actor storage factory gives every database it
opens a shipper: the database is kept in WAL mode with SQLite's own checkpointing off
(sqlite3_wal_autocheckpoint(0), SQLITE_DBCONFIG_NO_CKPT_ON_CLOSE) and synchronous=OFF, because this
machine's files are a cache and the write is durable once the node's peers hold its frames.
ActorSqlite's commit callback becomes the shipper's: it reads the frames committed since the last
one from the <database>-wal file, checks each as SQLite's own recovery does (the generation's salt,
a page number, the chained checksum) and takes only those up to the last commit frame, so a
transaction is handed on whole or not at all; sqlite3_wal_hook only records SQLite's exact frame
count, which says whether the WAL holds a frame the shipper has not read. The frames go to the
node's replicator over the descriptor COWORKERD_DO_WAL_FD, inherited at startup like the Server's
other channels (nothing is opened by path), and the promise the callback returns -- which the output
gate waits on, so every answer of the object waits with it -- resolves when the node confirms them.
The shipper has its own deadline, COWORKERD_DO_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_MS (5000, below workerd's 30 s output
gate timeout): past it the node is told which request it gave up on, and the write fails with a
jsg.Error naming STORAGE_UNAVAILABLE, so the caller gets a code rather than a reset object. A
checkpoint (TRUNCATE) is made only by the shipper, when the WAL has grown past
COWORKERD_DO_CHECKPOINT_BYTES (4 MiB) or the node says the object is idle, and only once every frame
read has been handed on, the connection is idle and the WAL holds nothing unread. afterReset()
(deleteAll) applies the settings again and tells the node to start the object from empty;
deleteFacetImpl() tells it a facet's files are gone, and cloneFacet() is refused for a shipped
namespace, because a copy of a database behind SQLite's back reaches no log. Opening a database that
already has a WAL reports where it starts, and the node breaks the object when that is past what it
confirmed: frames nobody confirmed must not be read. Without the descriptor nothing of this runs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

